    >
      View Pergola Hub &rarr;
    </a>
  </div>

  <!-- Editorial Deep Dive Content -->
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-12">
    <h2>1. The 1/4 Cantilever Overhang Rule</h2>
    <p>
      Pergolas feature decorative scroll-cut rafter tails that project past the support beams. In structural engineering, this overhang is an unbraced cantilever beam:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Golden 25% Ratio:</strong> The cantilever overhang (<code>L_cant</code>) must never exceed <strong>one-fourth (25%)</strong> of the clear span (<code>L_span</code>) between the support beams:
        <code>L_cant &le; L_span &divide; 4</code>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Practical Example:</strong> If your beams are spaced 12 feet apart (144 inches), the maximum safe decorative rafter overhang is <code>144 &divide; 4 = 36 inches (3.0 feet)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>What Happens if You Exceed 25%:</strong> Overhangs longer than 1/4 span act as powerful levers. During windstorms, aerodynamic uplift under the wide overhang exerts tremendous rotational torque, twisting rafters along the beam and tearing hurricane screws out of the wood.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Pergola Rafter Span Table (Open Air / Light Purlin Load)</h2>
    <p>
      These maximum clear spans apply to #2 or Better Western Red Cedar, Douglas Fir-Larch, or Southern Yellow Pine under open-air pergola configurations:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Rafter Lumber Size</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">16-Inch O.C. Spacing</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">24-Inch O.C. Spacing</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Max Safe Cantilever</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">2x6 Nominal</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">10 Feet - 4 Inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">9 Feet - 0 Inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">24 Inches (2.0 ft)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">2x8 Nominal</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">13 Feet - 6 Inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">11 Feet - 10 Inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">36 Inches (3.0 ft)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">2x10 Nominal</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">17 Feet - 2 Inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">15 Feet - 0 Inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">48 Inches (4.0 ft)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Main Support Beam Sizing Table (Post-to-Post Spans)</h2>
    <p>
      The two primary horizontal beams carry all the rafters and purlins. Always use doubled 2x lumber (sandwiching or notched into the 6x6 posts):
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Clear Span Between Posts</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Minimum Beam Specification</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Post Shoulder Notch Depth</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Hardware Fastener Spec</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Up to 8 Feet</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Double 2x8</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">1-1/2 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">(2) 1/2" Carriage Bolts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">8 to 12 Feet</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Double 2x10</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">1-1/2 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">(2) 1/2" Carriage Bolts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">12 to 16 Feet</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Double 2x12 or 4x10 Solid</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">1-1/2 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">(2) 5/8" Through-Bolts</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. The Triangle of Stability: 45-Degree Knee Bracing</h2>
    <p>
      In a freestanding structure, all lateral stability comes from the column-to-beam connection. Without diagonal braces, the four posts form 90-degree pin joints that easily fold flat under high winds.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Triangulation Rule:</strong> Install 4x4 or 4x6 timber braces cut at 45 degrees, measuring 30 to 36 inches along each leg.</li>
      <li><strong>Mechanical Lock:</strong> Fasten braces with two 3/8-inch x 6-inch structural timber screws (GRK RSS or Simpson SDWS) per connection. This converts flexible right angles into rigid geometric triangles that completely eliminate lateral sway.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- Trade Pro Field Note -->
  <div class="mb-10">
    <FieldNote
      title="Why 4x4 Posts Fail on Pergolas"
      trade="Structural Timber Design"
      content="Never use nominal 4x4 posts on a freestanding pergola taller than 7 feet. A 4x4 post only has an actual cross-section of 3.5 inches x 3.5 inches. Once you notch 1.5 inches out of the top to seat a beam, you leave a fragile 2.0-inch sliver of wood that can easily snap under lateral wind loads. 6x6 posts (actual 5.5 inches x 5.5 inches) provide 2.5 times the bending stiffness and leave a massive 4.0-inch structural center core after notching."
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Critical Costly Mistakes Box -->
  <div class="mb-12">
    <CostlyMistakes
      projectType="Pergola Sizing"
      mistakes={[
        {
          mistake: "Toe-Nailing Rafters Without Hurricane Ties",
          impact: "Driving three 3-inch nails at an angle through rafter sides provides less than 150 lbs of withdrawal resistance. A strong storm can easily vacuum the rafters off the beams.",
          prevention: "Always install Simpson Strong-Tie H2.5A or H1Z hurricane ties on every rafter-to-beam intersection."
        },
        {
          mistake: "Sizing Lumber for 'Zero Snow' in Northern States",
          impact: "While a pergola has no roof sheathing, wet, heavy snow accumulates on top 2x2 purlins and 2x8 rafters, adding 15 to 25 PSF of load that causes undersized 2x6 rafters to permanently bow.",
          prevention: "Use minimum 2x8 rafters for any span over 10 feet in cold climate zones."
        }
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Verified User Reviews -->
  <div class="mb-12">
    <CommunityReviews
      tradeTitle="Pergola Sizing Guide"
      reviews={[
        {
          author: "Travis M.",
          location: "Sacramento, CA",
          projectScope: "12x14 Douglas Fir Pergola",
          savingsClaim: "Saved $2,800 DIY",
          feedback: "I originally planned on using 2x6 rafters for a 13-foot span. This table showed me they would deflect under their own weight. Swapped to 2x8s spaced at 16 inches and the sightline across the patio is straight as an arrow.",
          keyTakeaway: "Use 2x8s for spans over 10 feet to eliminate visible mid-span sagging."
        }
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Author Reviewer Box -->
  <AuthorReviewer
    reviewerName="David Chen, PE"
    reviewerRole="Licensed Professional Structural Engineer"
    codeStandard="AWC National Design Specification (NDS) for Wood Construction"
    lastUpdated="September 2026"
  />

  <!-- Helpful Feedback Box -->
  <HelpfulFeedback />

</Layout>
